      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the least whole number of hours \\( h \\) greater than 4 hours such that \\( h \\) is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours. This means we need \\( h \\) and \\( h^2 \\) to be \"clock equivalent,\" i.e., the clock difference between them is a multiple of 12. Mathematically, this can be expressed as:\n\\[ h \\equiv h^2 \\pmod{12} \\]\nor equivalently,\n\\[ h^2 - h \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\\[ h(h-1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\nThis means that \\( h \\) and \\( h-1 \\) must include all the prime factors of 12, which are 2 and 3. Therefore, \\( h \\) must be of the form \\( 12k \\pm 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). We will check the values greater than 4 to find the smallest one that fits this form.\n1. For \\( h = 13 \\):\n\\[ 13 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\\[ 13^2 = 169 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\]\nSo, \\( 13 \\) is clock equivalent to \\( 13^2 = 169 \\).\n\n2. For \\( h = 19 \\):\n\\[ 19 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{12} \\]\nSince 7 is not congruent to 0 or 1 modulo 12, \\( 19 \\) is not clock equivalent to its square modulo 12.\n\n3. For \\( h = 23 \\):\n\\[ 23 \\equiv 11 \\pmod{12} \\]\nSince 11 is not congruent to 0 or 1 modulo 12, \\( 23 \\) is not clock equivalent to its square modulo 12.\n\n4. For \\( h = 29 \\):\n\\[ 29 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{12} \\]\nSince 5 is not congruent to 0 or 1 modulo 12, \\( 29 \\) is not clock equivalent to its square modulo 12.\n\nFrom the above, the only value of \\( h \\) that is greater than 4 and is clock equivalent to its square is \\( h = 13 \\). Therefore, the least whole number of hours that is greater than 4 hours and is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]",
